<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Rioja (Spain)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a> is an autonomous community of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logrono">Logroño</a> as its capital. It is famous for Rioja wine. The monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-millan-de-la-cogolla">San Millán de la Cogolla</a> preserve some of the earliest written glosses in Castilian and Basque. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the region was contested between the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, which secured it in 1176.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-rioja-spain.jpg" alt="La Rioja (Spain)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jynus · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_La_Rioja_(with_coat_of_arms).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
